  :adr/body "# hanrei.etzhayyim.com — Judgement/Case-Law Corpus Actor

## Context

Etzhayyim's legal topology so far comprises **houbun** (statute/legislation corpus, ADR-0091) and **ooyake** (public court registry, ADR-0090). Both are descriptive archives for law structure and judicial proceedings. The missing component is **precedent**: the actual judgement texts, case law, opinions, and their relationships to statutes, judges, and courts. This gap prevents cross-referencing a statute to its settled interpretation via prior judgements, and prevents tracing judicial trends.

Precedent is especially critical for:
- **Common law jurisdictions** (UK, USA, Canada, Australia, etc.) where judge-made law (case law) is formal source of law
- **Civil law jurisdictions** (France, Germany, Japan, etc.) where prior judgements are persuasive authority (not binding like common law) but heavily cited
- **International courts** (ICJ, ICC, ECJ) where published opinions establish doctrine
- **Comparative law research**: tracing how a single legal principle diverges across jurisdictions

The actor must **expose raw judgement corpus and analytics, not ranking or audit**. Constraints from ooyake (ADR-0090) G11 (\"no government ranking\") and danjo (audit) apply: activity tracking of judges and courts is descriptive (e.g., \"Judge X issued N decisions with Y% reversal rate\"), not normative (e.g., \"Judge X is problematic\").

## Scope

| In scope | Out of scope |
|---|---|
| Judgement text, headnotes, opinion, ruling, date | Rankings, ratings, \"problem judge\" tags |
| Case metadata: docket number, court, judges, parties | Sanctioning, disciplinary action |
| Citations between cases (cites/overrules/distinguishes) | Audit recommendations, court scorecards |
| Judge & court activity tracking (count, trends over time) | Normative judgement (\"good/bad decision\") |
| Statute references in judgements (links to houbun) | Court/judge performance targets, targets for reform |
| Topic/keyword tags, doctrine labels (eg \"tort\", \"contract\") | Public shaming, political ranking |
| Full-text search, boolean query, temporal analysis | Predictions about future judge behavior |
| DID topology (primary / jurisdiction:source / blake3 hash) | — |

## Decision

### A. Actor Identity & Persistence

- **Actor name**: `hanrei.etzhayyim.com` (judgement/precedent)
- **Persistence**: Kotoba Datomic (`com.etzhayyim.apps.hanrei.datomic.*`)
- **Deployment**: Etzhayyim hypercore + CF Worker (standard kotodama actor pattern)
- **Scope**: Global public judgements (no private/sealed case law initially)

### B. Entity Schema

**Core entities**:

- `:hanrei/case` — Judicial case metadata
  - `:case/docket-id` — Official case/docket number(s)
  - `:case/title` — Case name/style (parties & short description)
  - `:case/decision-date` — Publication date
  - `:case/decision-body` — Rendered HTML opinion text (full text)
  - `:case/headnote` — Brief summary (court-supplied or OCR)
  - `:case/court` — Reference edge to court (ooyake entity)
  - `:case/judges` — List of judge refs; order indicates role (presiding, concurring)
  - `:case/parties` — Plaintiff / defendant / appellant / respondent (string labels for now; may link to `ooyake/person` later)
  - `:case/keywords` — Topic tags (e.g., \"contract\", \"tort\", \"damages\", \"procedure\")
  - `:case/statute-refs` — Links to houbun entities cited
  - `:case/jurisdiction` — ISO 3166 country/state code
  - `:case/source` — Data source (court website, LexisNexis, Google Scholar, etc.)
  - `:case/source-url` — Canonical external link
  - `:case/blake3` — Content hash (for deduplication, DID component)

- `:hanrei/judge` — Judicial officer
  - `:judge/name` — Official name (from court registry, ooyake)
  - `:judge/court` — Primary court assignment (reference to ooyake entity)
  - `:judge/cases` — Reverse: all decisions authored
  - `:judge/activity` — `:judge/activity-entry` list (see below)

- `:hanrei/opinion` — Individual judge opinion/concurrence within a case
  - `:opinion/case` — Parent case
  - `:opinion/author` — Judge
  - `:opinion/type` — enum: \"majority\" | \"concurring\" | \"dissenting\"
  - `:opinion/text` — Full opinion text
  - `:opinion/headnote` — Synopsis

- `:hanrei/precedent-link` — Relationship between cases (citation)
  - `:link/from` — Citing case
  - `:link/to` — Cited case
  - `:link/type` — enum: \"cites\" | \"distinguishes\" | \"overrules\" | \"affirms\" | \"reverses\"
  - `:link/context` — Quote/passage from citing opinion that references cited case

- `:hanrei/judge-activity-entry` — Timestamped activity record
  - `:activity/date` — Month/year (discretization to prevent real-time tracking)
  - `:activity/judge` — Judge reference
  - `:activity/decisions-issued` — Count
  - `:activity/decisions-appealed` — Count
  - `:activity/appeals-reversed` — Count (of reversals by higher court)
  - `:activity/appeals-affirmed` — Count
  - Note: **descriptive only**. No \"problem count\" threshold or ranking. Analytics client decides interpretation.

### C. Reference Edges (Cross-Actor)

- `case → court` → ooyake (`com.etzhayyim.apps.ooyake.datomic.court`)
- `judge → court` → ooyake
- `judge → person` → ooyake (`com.etzhayyim.apps.ooyake.datomic.person`, once ooyake supports judge linking)
- `case → statute` → houbun (`com.etzhayyim.apps.houbun.datomic.statute`)

### D. DID Topology

3-layer identifier for portable case references:

```
hanrei://primary:blake3-<content-hash>
         /jurisdiction:source:JPN:Tokyo-District:2025-001234
         /jurisdiction:source:USA:CA-9th-Circuit:123-45678
```

- **Layer 1 (primary)**: `blake3-<hash>` — Content-addressable identity (survives re-source from different vendor)
- **Layer 2 (jurisdiction:source)**: `<jurisdiction>:<source-system>` — Canonical reference (court's own docket system)
- Layer 3 (vendor): URL/vendor-specific identifier (fallback, may disappear)

DID resolution:
- Try Layer 2 first (native court registry query). If unavailable, fall back to Layer 1 (blake3 content match across sources).
- URLs are ephemeral; do not rely on them as canonical identity.

### E. Content Sources (Phase 1)

Planned ingestion tiers:

1. **Japan**: 最高裁判例情報システム (Supreme Court Precedent Information System, public API), Tokyo District, Osaka High Court, IP courts
2. **USA**: Google Scholar (free, full-text, 1900+), RECAP (Federal), state court sites
3. **EU**: CURIA (ECJ, GCEU; European Court of Justice), EUR-Lex CELEX (legislation references)
4. **International**: ICJ, ICC, ICtHY, WTO Appellate Body

**Not included initially**: Trial court opinions (most jurisdictions, not archived publicly), sealed/confidential cases, legal memoranda (not published precedent).

### F. Eguards: What We Do NOT Do

**Cite (ADR-0090 ooyake G11 compliance)**: No public ranking, scoring, or aggregation that implies judicial quality:
- ❌ \"Judge reversal rate scorecard\" (implies criticism)
- ❌ \"Top 10 most-cited judges\"
- ❌ \"Court efficiency rating\"
- ✅ \"Judge X issued 150 decisions, of which 12 were appealed and 4 reversed (2.7% reversal rate)\" — raw data, no value judgment

**Cite (danjo audit scope separation)**: Audit findings belong to independent danjo (弾正) actor, not hanrei:
- ❌ \"Judge X violated procedural rules in Case Y\" (audit conclusion)
- ✅ \"Reversal rate time series\" (descriptive trend)

**Scope boundary with houbun**:
- hanrei = precedent (cases, opinions, citations)
- houbun = legislation (statutes, codes, regulations, gazette)
- houbun may reference cases; hanrei may reference statutes. No content duplication.

## Consequences

**Positive**:
- Legal researchers can now query case law via standard tools (datomic queries, full-text, DID lookup).
- Comparative law analysis across jurisdictions (query API: \"cases in Japan citing Article 320, and US cases with similar holdings\").
- Basis for future tools (appellate trends, doctrine evolution, precedent divergence across regions) without ranking bias.
- Integration with houbun (statute) and ooyake (court) completes the trias politica architecture (legislature / executive / judiciary data models).

**Neutral**:
- Corpus maintenance is ongoing (court sites evolve, OCR quality varies, metadata incomplete for older cases).
- DID resolution Layer 1↔2 mapping requires periodic sync with source courts.
- Full-text indexing for large corpus (100K+ cases) requires document store (ElasticSearch, Whoosh, or Datomic full-text plugins).

**Risks / Guardrails**:
- **Reversal rate / activity metrics can be misused for ranking**. Documentation and API design must make clear that raw metrics are value-neutral. Policy enforcement: (1) code comments in activity schema, (2) API docs example disclaimers, (3) optional `etzhayyim.policy.non-ranking` constraint for clients.
- **OCR errors in historical case law** may introduce false citations. Versioning strategy: keep source URL + OCR version, allow client-side correction without modifying canonical hanrei entity.
- **Uneven coverage across jurisdictions** (common law rich, civil law sparse, developing economies minimal). Phase 1 focuses on 3 regions; expansion requires resource. Version/source metadata must be transparent.

## Alternatives Considered

| Option | Why not |
|---|---|
| **Replicate houbun/ooyake models (just copy the actor pattern)** | Would work, but misses opportunity for precedent-specific features (DID 3-layer, citation links, opinion separation, activity tracking) |
| **Merge hanrei into houbun** | No. Legislation and precedent are distinct data models; mixing obscures the boundary and makes querying hard |
| **Merge hanrei into ooyake** | No. Courts are institutions; judgements are artifacts. A court actor should expose registry (parties, addresses, judges), not all cases the court published |
| **Use a separate triple-store (RDF/SPARQL) instead of Datomic** | Datomic is SSoT for etzhayyim actor data (ADR-0036 worker-direct). RDF would require dual maintenance and breaks actor persistence pattern. Keep as single Datomic instance per actor |
| **Include audit findings (appeal reversal interpreted as \"bad decision\")** | Violates ooyake G11 and danjo scope boundary. Keep hanrei descriptive. Let policy actor (danjo) make normative calls. |
| **Public judge \"problem scores\" based on reversal rate** | Same violation. Public shaming is not etzhayyim's role |
| **Include sealed/confidential cases** | Some jurisdictions prohibit publication. Violate local law. Start with public cases only; if sealed corpus access needed, route through separate secure actor with special auth. |

